    {
      "id": "card.us_history.020.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Self-rule, and who was left out",
      "body": "Virginia’s first elected assembly and its first recorded Africans both arrived in 1619, a year before Plymouth was founded. That pairing runs through the whole chapter. Colonial assemblies controlled taxes and even the governor’s salary. But voters were usually white men who owned property, while Southern plantations relied on enslaved Africans. Native nations pushed back: Metacom listed the land his people were losing, and thousands of Native people were killed in the war that followed. In 1763 Britain won the East, and a debt that had nearly doubled."
    },
